369
00:23:46,836 --> 00:23:56,461
[SPEAKER_01]: Therefore, I was thinking that you must... that by understanding anicca, you come to understanding dukkha, and then... You go to the next one to understand anicca.

370
00:23:56,501 --> 00:23:57,101
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no.

371
00:23:57,582 --> 00:24:01,644
[SPEAKER_00]: It's one insight that you can develop to have these three characteristics.

372
00:24:02,684 --> 00:24:11,758
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you might not necessarily be equally discerning all the characteristics, but you see them all to some extent, hence the insight is there.

373
00:24:12,219 --> 00:24:13,601
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the same with the Four Noble Truths.

374
00:24:14,021 --> 00:24:16,305
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just four aspects of the one thing.

375
00:24:17,287 --> 00:24:21,250
[SPEAKER_00]: Not like one, and then you go to the second, and then, after a few days, you reach the third.

376
00:24:21,790 --> 00:24:21,950
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

377
00:24:21,990 --> 00:24:28,415
[SPEAKER_00]: It's basically just four ways of laying out every every aspect of that one insight, which is freedom from suffering.

378
00:24:28,835 --> 00:24:29,015
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

379
00:24:29,195 --> 00:24:32,177
[SPEAKER_00]: The one who is free from suffering knows what the suffering is.

380
00:24:32,217 --> 00:24:34,819
[SPEAKER_00]: If he doesn't know what the suffering is, he wouldn't be able to be free from it.

381
00:24:35,780 --> 00:24:37,601
[SPEAKER_00]: He knows how that suffering arises.

382
00:24:37,661 --> 00:24:39,182
[SPEAKER_00]: He knows how that suffering came to be.

383
00:24:39,822 --> 00:24:41,463
[SPEAKER_00]: He knows how that suffering ceases.

384
00:24:41,584 --> 00:24:42,784
[SPEAKER_00]: And he knows what he needs to do.

385
00:24:43,645 --> 00:24:44,946
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's all in one insight.

386
00:24:45,648 --> 00:24:56,113
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you want to teach that one insight to somebody, this is the ultimate way, the most accurate, precise way to lay it out, as for aspects that need to be covered.

387
00:24:56,694 --> 00:24:59,735
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why there's another sort of... Oh, I forgot.

388
00:24:59,975 --> 00:25:14,843
[SPEAKER_00]: When it kind of breaks it down to, like, three, you need to develop three, and the kind of, eh... Four Noble Truths become, like, twelve path or steps, so to speak, where every truth needs to be developed fully.

389
00:25:15,685 --> 00:25:19,207
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you realize...

390
00:25:19,227 --> 00:25:21,469
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you have the four aspects.

391
00:25:21,809 --> 00:25:27,132
[SPEAKER_00]: To fully develop four aspects, I... like... and each aspect can be developed three-fold.

392
00:25:27,413 --> 00:25:28,133
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

393
00:25:28,213 --> 00:25:29,274
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you get twelve.

394
00:25:29,814 --> 00:25:31,495
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in that sense, you get four and twelve.

395
00:25:31,515 --> 00:25:32,776
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's not like one, two, three, four, five.

396
00:25:32,796 --> 00:25:33,737
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just one,

397
00:25:34,577 --> 00:25:35,018
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, yes.

398
00:25:35,038 --> 00:25:36,999
[SPEAKER_00]: But some aspects can be more prominent.

399
00:25:37,019 --> 00:25:37,380
[SPEAKER_00]: Can't be.

400
00:26:02,342 --> 00:26:02,842
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, sure.

401
00:26:02,862 --> 00:26:04,703
[SPEAKER_00]: But you might be clear in some aspects.

402
00:26:05,124 --> 00:26:08,506
[SPEAKER_00]: But that means, basically, there is still some ignorance left.

403
00:26:08,526 --> 00:26:12,048
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you fully develop all four aspects, you'll be an arahant.

404
00:26:12,768 --> 00:26:16,250
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means Ārya does not have all four aspects fully developed.

405
00:26:16,810 --> 00:26:18,071
[SPEAKER_00]: He has them to some extent.

406
00:26:18,331 --> 00:26:19,312
[SPEAKER_00]: But he has them.

407
00:26:19,732 --> 00:26:21,173
[SPEAKER_00]: He has the Four Noble Truths.

408
00:26:22,013 --> 00:26:24,096
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

409
00:26:24,136 --> 00:26:25,437
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, no, that's just an approach.

410
00:26:25,497 --> 00:26:26,678
[SPEAKER_01]: That's just one approach.

411
00:26:26,858 --> 00:26:28,240
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

412
00:26:28,340 --> 00:26:30,302
[SPEAKER_00]: No, it's not necessary in a sense.

413
00:26:30,322 --> 00:26:33,045
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to... As I said, it's not... it's not a fixed method.

414
00:26:33,225 --> 00:26:33,465
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

415
00:26:33,645 --> 00:26:35,727
[SPEAKER_00]: It's... there's just one goal.

416
00:26:35,807 --> 00:26:38,110
[SPEAKER_00]: As the Buddha said, he only teaches suffering and freedom from it.

417
00:26:38,690 --> 00:26:43,796
[SPEAKER_00]: As the ocean has only the taste of salt, no matter where it is, so he teaches freedom from suffering.

418
00:26:43,816 --> 00:26:44,156
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

419
00:26:44,772 --> 00:26:52,481
[SPEAKER_00]: So, no matter what approach you take, no matter through what sutta you arrive at understanding, no matter how different it might sound, those are not all methods you have to cover.

420
00:26:52,501 --> 00:26:53,762
[SPEAKER_00]: They all point at the same thing.

421
00:26:53,822 --> 00:26:56,024
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you arrive at the same thing, that's all it matters.

422
00:26:57,126 --> 00:27:02,532
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if somebody arrives at the same thing through contemplation of the nature of suffering, craving in regard to it and so on,

423
00:27:03,012 --> 00:27:04,954
[SPEAKER_00]: Then he becomes free from suffering.

424
00:27:05,074 --> 00:27:06,896
[SPEAKER_00]: He becomes an arahant.

425
00:27:06,916 --> 00:27:07,296
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

426
00:27:07,557 --> 00:27:16,265
[SPEAKER_00]: If somebody, you know, with a different mind, arrives to understanding of this simultaneous principle, which is patitsa upādāna, of the actual and peripheral, he becomes free from suffering.

427
00:27:16,285 --> 00:27:17,086
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the same result.

428
00:27:18,448 --> 00:27:26,493
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why sometimes, that's what the Navarra meant, that sometimes even people with the right view might actually hesitate if they were to describe Pāṭicca Samupādā as twelve factors.

429
00:27:27,094 --> 00:27:35,239
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's a subtle thing to see, and they might have arrived differently at the freedom from suffering, and then they never thought of Pāṭicca Samupādā in that relevant manner.

430
00:27:35,560 --> 00:27:38,942
[SPEAKER_00]: They thought it, oh, it's just some explanation, and they don't care about it.

431
00:27:38,962 --> 00:27:39,122
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

432
00:27:39,142 --> 00:27:42,004
[SPEAKER_00]: Because they arrived at freedom from suffering through some completely different approach.

433
00:27:42,224 --> 00:27:42,464
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

434
00:27:42,785 --> 00:27:43,785
[SPEAKER_00]: Again, from the suttas.

435
00:27:44,065 --> 00:27:44,306
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

436
00:27:44,506 --> 00:27:45,246
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why, that's why

437
00:27:48,088 --> 00:27:52,489
[SPEAKER_00]: Some have arrived only through like a single kind of approach that they really committed to and had faith in.

438
00:27:52,990 --> 00:27:58,171
[SPEAKER_00]: Some arrived through discernment of many different approaches that kind of brought mind to fulfillment.

439
00:27:58,331 --> 00:28:00,532
[SPEAKER_00]: And their wisdom, basis for wisdom, is greater.

440
00:28:00,552 --> 00:28:01,672
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why they can teach better.

441
00:28:01,692 --> 00:28:03,833
[SPEAKER_00]: They can understand better.

442
00:28:04,313 --> 00:28:05,353
[SPEAKER_00]: But not all of them can.

443
00:28:05,954 --> 00:28:08,314
[SPEAKER_00]: But some of them can only teach in a manner that they reached.

444
00:28:08,774 --> 00:28:09,895
[SPEAKER_00]: But it doesn't mean that they're less.

445
00:28:10,315 --> 00:28:14,996
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the Buddha said it doesn't matter how you reach it or how wide you have to circle in order to reach it.

446
00:28:15,016 --> 00:28:16,177
[SPEAKER_00]: The point is to reach it.

447
00:28:16,777 --> 00:28:17,317
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's why the

448
00:28:17,676 --> 00:28:26,580
[SPEAKER_00]: Faith type, the wisdom type, and the kind of a meditation sort of translation, meditation type, one through samādhi.

449
00:28:27,541 --> 00:28:28,861
[SPEAKER_00]: None of them is better than the other.

450
00:28:29,922 --> 00:28:34,244
[SPEAKER_00]: The one who is the best is the one who becomes an arahant, and any of them can become an arahant on the basis of these three things.

451
00:28:35,645 --> 00:28:41,868
[SPEAKER_00]: So it's not like, oh, he's a better arahant because he arrived through wisdom, and then he's a worse arahant because he arrived through faith.

452
00:28:41,968 --> 00:28:42,128
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

453
00:28:42,228 --> 00:28:42,948
[SPEAKER_00]: Both are arahants.

454
00:28:43,008 --> 00:28:43,809
[SPEAKER_00]: That's all that matters.

455
00:28:45,970 --> 00:28:46,250
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

456
00:28:47,261 --> 00:28:51,664
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's what problem is.

457
00:28:51,845 --> 00:28:55,327
[SPEAKER_01]: And we were also talking about this anicca.

458
00:28:55,347 --> 00:28:57,289
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you say it again?

459
00:28:57,489 --> 00:28:59,170
[SPEAKER_00]: What is actually anicca?

460
00:28:59,290 --> 00:29:09,118
[SPEAKER_00]: Again, it would be hard to undo the notion of material sort of scientific impermanence.

461
00:29:09,978 --> 00:29:14,101
[SPEAKER_00]: Because when people say it's impermanent, they expect it's destruction.

462
00:29:15,060 --> 00:29:17,561
[SPEAKER_00]: And they often use that as a proof of impermanence.

463
00:29:17,701 --> 00:29:22,463
[SPEAKER_00]: Once something has been destroyed, you say, yes, truly, look how these things are all impermanent.

464
00:29:23,144 --> 00:29:35,829
[SPEAKER_00]: But you... not just that that doesn't make any any kind of relevant sense for the freedom of the sense of self, but also you can't even apply it in that sense, because it would require you to kill yourself, to destroy yourself, to see yourself as not yourself.

465
00:29:37,150 --> 00:29:41,132
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in order to see you're not self, you actually require the phenomenon of self.

466
00:29:42,232 --> 00:29:44,153
[SPEAKER_00]: To see it as not belonging to you.

467
00:29:44,929 --> 00:29:52,356
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you destroy it, even before you see it, means you completely, sort of... well, you entirely, just entirely block your own progress.

468
00:29:52,376 --> 00:29:56,940
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't possibly... you remove any basis for discernment of that particular thing.

469
00:29:57,740 --> 00:29:59,442
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you should really think...

470
00:30:00,875 --> 00:30:06,317
[SPEAKER_00]: Anicca, as I said, it's a kind of wide basis in Sanskrit.

471
00:30:06,337 --> 00:30:09,178
[SPEAKER_00]: Anicca means everlasting.

472
00:30:09,559 --> 00:30:15,901
[SPEAKER_00]: But even the definition, by the way, of permanent, if you look at the English definition, means lasting until it comes to an end.

473
00:30:16,521 --> 00:30:19,002
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it has permanent presence until it disappears.

474
00:30:19,702 --> 00:30:21,663
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the whole notion of eternal,

475
00:30:22,123 --> 00:30:27,464
[SPEAKER_00]: It gets kind of preached into that sense of self, and then, oh, eternal, non-eternal, and so on.

476
00:30:28,004 --> 00:30:29,965
[SPEAKER_00]: But even that can be too abstract for people.

477
00:30:30,305 --> 00:30:30,445
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

478
00:30:30,665 --> 00:30:35,426
[SPEAKER_00]: So, on a more practical level, if you want to look at anicca, it means, basically, out of your control.

479
00:30:35,606 --> 00:30:42,628
[SPEAKER_00]: That which is present, but cannot be owned, cannot be truly in your possession, in your mastery.

480
00:30:42,928 --> 00:30:43,168
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

481
00:30:44,208 --> 00:30:48,633
[SPEAKER_00]: And you recognize everything exercises that degree of resistance to some degree.

482
00:30:49,094 --> 00:30:54,961
[SPEAKER_00]: Everything exercises resistance to some degree to you, which means everything shares the nature of anicca to you.

483
00:30:55,982 --> 00:30:59,647
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's that varying degrees of anicca that confuse people.

484
00:31:00,420 --> 00:31:10,949
[SPEAKER_00]: In thinking that things that are kind of exercising less of a independence, kind of resistance attitude towards you, you confuse them as yours, because it's resisting you less.

485
00:31:11,530 --> 00:31:15,153
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's not a question of not resisting you at all.

486
00:31:15,313 --> 00:31:20,758
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just a question of, basically, things are impermanent and less impermanent, but they're still impermanent.

487
00:31:22,199 --> 00:31:22,760
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words,

488
00:31:23,513 --> 00:31:24,533
[SPEAKER_00]: Things are out of your control.

489
00:31:24,834 --> 00:31:30,376
[SPEAKER_00]: Some of them more so, some of them less so, but fundamentally, none of them is fully in your control.

490
00:31:31,236 --> 00:31:39,580
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's all it's... all it takes, just to recognize that then, ah, so then this whole notion of self, there is the notion of absolute control.

491
00:31:40,020 --> 00:31:42,381
[SPEAKER_00]: The reason self cannot be based upon partial control.

492
00:31:43,121 --> 00:31:47,163
[SPEAKER_00]: If self recognizes partial control elsewhere, it means it's taking full control somewhere else.

493
00:31:47,803 --> 00:31:49,124
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why it's established upon.

494
00:31:49,844 --> 00:31:56,688
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you might dismiss everything saying, I know this is mine because I don't control it, but maybe that very thought is something you think you control.

495
00:31:56,708 --> 00:31:58,850
[SPEAKER_00]: So, your sense of self remains lingering to that.

496
00:31:59,270 --> 00:31:59,490
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

497
00:32:00,051 --> 00:32:06,135
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why you need to include a peripheral, the general nature whereby you kind of scoop it all up and then discern it within that.

498
00:32:06,355 --> 00:32:06,595
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

499
00:32:07,142 --> 00:32:12,724
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you keep chasing where yourself is established, you won't find it, because it's always going to slip to the next thing.

500
00:32:12,744 --> 00:32:12,944
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

501
00:32:13,765 --> 00:32:22,188
[SPEAKER_00]: So, again, anicca, as that Sanskrit anicca also has one of the meanings, is dear and internal, personal, personal.

502
00:32:22,868 --> 00:32:24,989
[SPEAKER_00]: So, contemplate the nature of personal.

503
00:32:25,289 --> 00:32:26,930
[SPEAKER_00]: Take something that you... oh, this is personal.

504
00:32:27,610 --> 00:32:29,531
[SPEAKER_00]: If you were to say that, what do you refer to?

505
00:32:29,871 --> 00:32:31,452
[SPEAKER_00]: What do I refer to as personal?

506
00:32:32,205 --> 00:32:42,332
[SPEAKER_00]: And you would see, kind of, if you were to describe it phenomenologically, that experience, you would be referring to something that's close, almost inside, in relation to the outside.

507
00:32:42,392 --> 00:32:43,533
[SPEAKER_00]: It's something right here.

508
00:32:44,713 --> 00:32:46,194
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's why it's personal.

509
00:32:47,335 --> 00:32:48,696
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why you took possession of it.

510
00:32:48,716 --> 00:32:49,697
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why you guarded it.

511
00:32:50,337 --> 00:32:51,778
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why you kind of sheltered it.

512
00:32:52,479 --> 00:32:58,963
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you recognize that none of the things can be personal in that sense, that's how you disown it.

513
00:33:00,015 --> 00:33:01,035
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what anicca is.

514
00:33:01,656 --> 00:33:14,279
[SPEAKER_00]: By recognizing that none of that can be personal, that any of that can come to an end at any time, and you recognize that while the thing is still present, that's how you sort of... well, you recognize danger.

515
00:33:14,800 --> 00:33:19,601
[SPEAKER_00]: You're holding on to something that will sort of pull you to death because of your holding.

516
00:33:19,661 --> 00:33:24,062
[SPEAKER_00]: Not because you, you know, you have to die, just because you're gripping on to it.

517
00:33:24,783 --> 00:33:25,643
[SPEAKER_00]: So you recognize that

518
00:33:26,150 --> 00:33:32,294
[SPEAKER_00]: All these things that I hold dear, for that very reason, just for that very reason, can be a result of my demise.

519
00:33:32,394 --> 00:33:38,017
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's very dangerous, but at the same time, it becomes very liberating recognizing that, because then you let go of it.

520
00:33:38,637 --> 00:33:44,400
[SPEAKER_00]: So, even if you keep... remain surrounded with your possessions and stuff, none of them will be regarded on that personal level.

521
00:33:45,301 --> 00:33:48,723
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if something happens, if you lose your house, if you lose this, if you lose your friend,

522
00:33:49,748 --> 00:33:50,128
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

523
00:33:50,289 --> 00:33:59,617
[SPEAKER_00]: The mind will not be affected, it won't be shattered by that, because it has withdrawn, recalled away from it, before it disappeared, before it took you away.

524
00:34:01,584 --> 00:34:02,505
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what anicca is.

525
00:34:02,525 --> 00:34:08,349
[SPEAKER_00]: That's where, again, automatically, by recognizing anicca properly, you recognize the suffering in it.

526
00:34:08,790 --> 00:34:10,151
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, just for that reason.

527
00:34:10,771 --> 00:34:14,915
[SPEAKER_00]: Not because it might be a very good thing, might be a very happy thing, family, children, all that.

528
00:34:15,135 --> 00:34:15,755
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

529
00:34:15,775 --> 00:34:23,321
[SPEAKER_00]: By automatically, or simultaneously better, by recognizing suffering, you don't regard that as dear, you don't regard it as personal, you don't regard that as mine.

530
00:34:23,502 --> 00:34:23,722
Yes.

531
00:34:24,222 --> 00:34:24,602
[SPEAKER_00]: Cannot.

532
00:34:25,142 --> 00:34:25,582
[SPEAKER_00]: Possibly.

533
00:34:25,923 --> 00:34:33,405
[SPEAKER_00]: Because in order to have that attitude of mine, that lingering notion of mine, belonging to me, you need to see it as personal.

534
00:34:33,445 --> 00:34:34,625
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to see it as dear.

535
00:34:34,945 --> 00:34:38,806
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to see it as worthy, as pleasurable, as non-dangerous.

536
00:34:39,747 --> 00:34:40,067
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

537
00:34:40,467 --> 00:34:40,707
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

538
00:34:41,027 --> 00:34:51,490
[SPEAKER_01]: So, can you say that actually, you know, the... what you consider to be self is not yourself, if I summarize

539
00:34:52,301 --> 00:34:52,981
[SPEAKER_01]: The whole point.

540
00:34:53,281 --> 00:34:54,682
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the only point is just...

541
00:34:54,722 --> 00:35:01,785
[SPEAKER_00]: So, to summarize even slightly more accurately, but you can see that, that which you take to be yourself, is a real thing.

542
00:35:02,745 --> 00:35:03,846
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I wanted to ask.

543
00:35:04,383 --> 00:35:08,445
[SPEAKER_00]: You just have to remove your sense of ownership in regard to that notion.

544
00:35:08,605 --> 00:35:09,065
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

545
00:35:09,085 --> 00:35:14,308
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that notion is still basically there, but you don't consider... See, but you can't see that notion directly.

546
00:35:14,368 --> 00:35:18,390
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why this whole point of training your mind in peripheral recognition.

547
00:35:18,530 --> 00:35:18,790
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

548
00:35:19,210 --> 00:35:26,514
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how you start including that vague notion of self, that vague notion of ownership, that vague notion of master external to this.

549
00:35:27,334 --> 00:35:31,837
[SPEAKER_00]: You start including into that peripheral that you see it's present while the actual has arisen.

550
00:35:32,057 --> 00:35:32,297
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

551
00:35:33,341 --> 00:35:42,323
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if the mind becomes established in that peripheral, you realize self never appears as a direct object, because it wouldn't be the self.

552
00:35:42,483 --> 00:35:42,683
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

553
00:35:42,823 --> 00:35:45,644
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, in order to tackle the self, you need to see the self.

554
00:35:45,864 --> 00:35:46,084
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

555
00:35:46,184 --> 00:35:47,484
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to see the phenomenal self.

556
